A caller that passes the kubeconfig text instead of a path (Net::Async::Kubernetes->new(kubeconfig => $yaml), the same through Kubernetes::REST) gets "Kubeconfig not found: <the whole YAML> and not running in-cluster" plus an "Unsuccessful stat on filename containing newline" warning. The message (lib/Kubernetes/REST/Kubeconfig.pm, _kubeconfig_paths_string, around line 265; the stat around line 66) then carries every token and client key of that kubeconfig into logs.

Seen offline while writing the consumer skill perl-net-async-kubernetes (p5-net-async-kubernetes k82).

Done when: a path value that holds a newline (or does not look like a path) dies with a message that names the mistake ("kubeconfig expects a path, got a document") and quotes none of its content; a test pins that no secret from the document appears in the error.

Hub decision (perl-developer-bb, 2026-11-06), not a maintainer decision: a kubeconfig value that holds a line break or does not look like a path dies with a message that names the mistake ('kubeconfig expects a path, got a document') and quotes nothing of its content. The stat on such a value goes, and with it the 'Unsuccessful stat' warning. The test pins that no token and no key from the document appears in the message.
Coordinator's choice (mine, not the maintainer's): 'does not look like a path' = the value contains \n, \r or NUL, or starts (after leading whitespace) with '{' or '[' (JSON) or '---' (YAML document marker). Checked on the raw value before the path_sep split - a string as a whole, an arrayref per entry - so it also covers a document in KUBECONFIG. The message names where the value came from (kubeconfig_path / KUBECONFIG), never the value.

worker: fix in lib/Kubernetes/REST/Kubeconfig.pm, uncommitted. kubeconfig_paths builder rejects a document (newline, CR, NUL, or leading {, [, ---) on the raw value / per arrayref entry before the split; croaks "kubeconfig expects a path, got a document in kubeconfig_path" or "... in the environment variable KUBECONFIG", quoting nothing, no stat, no in-cluster fallback. Source tracked by private _kubeconfig_path_from_env, set only by the kubeconfig_path default. t/76 33/33; prove -lr t/ Files=74 Tests=940 PASS. Follow-ups in the worker report.

Coordinator's contract extension (mine): the leading-marker check also runs on every entry after the path_sep split, so '/a/config:{json}' is a document too (it leaked the token through 'Kubeconfig not found'). The croak names the caller's line when reached through the lazy builders (house pattern, _carp_past_builders). Both pinned in t/76.

worker, follow-up: (1) the document check now runs on every entry after the path_sep split, so "path:{json}" (kubeconfig_path or KUBECONFIG) dies with the same message; for newline/CR/NUL this is equivalent to checking the raw value, since the split leaves them in place. (2) the croak uses local our @CARP_NOT = (Method::Generate::Accessor::_Generated), scoped to this one croak, following _carp_past_builders, so it names the caller line through api/contexts/current_context_name. t/76 58/58; prove -lr t/ Files=74 Tests=965 PASS.

